# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch blocks for it are dead code
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`**

## Why Thread.Abort Was Removed

It was inherently unsafe — it could interrupt code at any point (including inside `finally` blocks or `lock` statements), causing corrupted state, orphaned locks, and resource leaks.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask Stop(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        // Signal cooperative cancellation
        await _cts.CancelAsync();

        // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Abort
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)))
        {
            // Log warning: worker did not exit within timeout
            // Unlike Abort(), we cannot forcibly kill it — by design
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Guidance |
|---------|----------|
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | Rewrite `GetNextItem()` → `GetNextItemAsync(CancellationToken)`. If wrapping legacy sync code, use `token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` at checkpoints. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Always store the `Task` and observe exceptions, or they silently vanish. |
| **`CancellationTokenSource` not disposed** | It holds OS handles — always dispose it. |
| **Assuming instant stop** | Cooperative cancellation means the worker only stops at the *next* cancellation checkpoint. Design tight check intervals. |
| **Replacing `volatile bool` with just a token** | `CancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested` is thread-safe and replaces the `volatile bool` pattern entirely. |
| **Using `Thread.Sleep` for timeout** | Use `Task.WhenAny` + `Task.Delay` (or `WaitAsync(timeout)` in .NET 8) instead. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Code Async

For legacy sync code that truly can't be refactored:

```csharp
private void ProcessLoopSync(CancellationToken token)
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // explicit checkpoint

        var item = GetNextItem(); // still sync
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // another checkpoint

        ProcessItem(item);
    }
}
```

This is the closest safe equivalent to the old abort pattern — cancellation only happens at well-defined points, avoiding the corruption risks that motivated removing `Thread.Abort`.
